```

And `nl_msg_put_unspec` calls `nullable_memcpy(ptr, data, size)` where `data` is `metadata` and `size` is `md_size`.

The issue is: `md_size` could be larger than `NSH_CTX_HDRS_MAX_LEN` if `ofpbuf_put_hex` causes the buffer to be reallocated (moved to heap), but `md_size` is set to `mdlen + padding` where `mdlen` is the length of the hex-decoded data. However, `ofpbuf_use_stub` uses the stack buffer initially, and if the data grows beyond the stub size, `ofpbuf_put_hex` will reallocate to heap memory. But `md_size` would still reflect the actual data length.

Wait, let me re-read the code more carefully:

```c
struct ofpbuf b;
char buf[512];
size_t mdlen, padding;
if (ovs_scan_len(s, &n, "md2=0x%511[0-9a-fA-F]", buf)) {
    ofpbuf_use_stub(&b, metadata, NSH_CTX_HDRS_MAX_LEN);
    ofpbuf_put_hex(&b, buf, &mdlen);
    /* Pad metadata to 4 bytes. */
    padding = PAD_SIZE(mdlen, 4);
    if (padding > 0) {
        ofpbuf_push_zeros(&b, padding);
    }
    md_size = mdlen + padding;
    ofpbuf_uninit(&b);
    continue;
}
```

The `ofpbuf_use_stub` uses `metadata` (stack buffer of 248 bytes) as the initial buffer. `ofpbuf_put_hex` decodes hex string into binary and appends to the buffer. If the decoded data exceeds 248 bytes, `ofpbuf_put_hex` will dynamically allocate a new buffer (via malloc), copy the data, and the original stack buffer is no longer used. Then `ofpbuf_uninit` frees the malloc'd buffer.

But `md_size` is set to `mdlen + padding` which is the actual data length. Then `nsh_key_to_attr` is called with `metadata` (the stack buffer) and `md_size` (which could be > 248). Inside `nsh_key_to_attr`, `nl_msg_put_unspec(buf, OVS_NSH_KEY_ATTR_MD2, metadata, md_size)` is called, which does `nullable_memcpy(ptr, metadata, md_size)` - this reads `md_size` bytes from `metadata` which is only 248 bytes on the stack!

So the bug is: when the hex-decoded MD2 data exceeds `NSH_CTX_HDRS_MAX_LEN` (248 bytes), `ofpbuf_put_hex` reallocates internally, but `md_size` still reflects the full size. Then `nsh_key_to_attr` reads `md_size` bytes from the original stack buffer `metadata`, causing a stack buffer over-read.

